The note says that an element is a consistent closed set of tokens and that a map between domains is an input/output relation \(u\, f\, v\) on finite consistent sets, because a finite set of premises may be required for entailment, and it points to Definitions 5.1 and 5.2 and Propositions 5.3–5.6. The only computation it gives is that the constant map is immediate once \(u\,(\mathrm{const}\, b)\, v\) means \(v \subseteq b\), with the remark that the same notation is used for a map out of \(\mathbf{A}\) and a map out of \(\mathbf{D}\). `ApproximableMap` is Definition 5.1: related finite sets are consistent, the empty sets are related, the relation is closed under union on the right, and a stronger input entailment together with a weaker output entailment preserves the relation. `toElement` is Definition 5.2, the element of those tokens \(Y\) for which some finite subset of the input is related to the singleton of \(Y\). `exists_rel_of_subset_image` builds that finite witness by induction on the output set, from the empty relation and, at an insertion, from the union of a singleton witness and the inductive witness, using monotonicity and closure under union on the right; `singleton_funion_eq` identifies the singleton union with insertion, and `Basic` records that membership in the image is this witness. `toElement_mono` is monotonicity of the image, and `ext` equates maps that relate the same pairs. The constant-map reading \(v \subseteq b\), and the double use of \(\mathrm{const}\, b\), are not stated. `Factoid46/Basic` states that the basic open of a finite token set is the set of elements containing those tokens, that the domain of elements is \(T_0\), and that approximable maps correspond to Scott-continuous maps by sending a continuous map to the relation that holds when the output finite set lies in the image of the closure of the input; that correspondence is not a claim of this note. **Theorem:** partial — Definitions 5.1 and 5.2 match the note's relation on finite sets and the element generated from it, and \(u\,(\mathrm{const}\, b)\, v \iff v \subseteq b\) is absent. **Proof:** no — the note's argument is the reading of the constant relation, and the imported arguments are the well-definedness of the image.
